B.Tx=new A.Z(57699,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.SK=new A.bc("red_dot","The Red Dot","Notification badges are colored to stand out against any background, creating a visual tension that you feel compelled to clear by opening the app.","Apple & Facebook Growth Teams (2009)","Turn off badge notifications for all non-essential apps.",B.hi,B.Tx,"Dscout, 'Mobile Touches' study (2016)","Dscout researchers counted an average of 2,617 phone touches per user per day.",!1)
B.Sv=new A.bc("typing_indicator","Typing Indicators","The 'someone is typing' indicator creates an open loop: you know a reply is coming, so you keep waiting on the screen instead of closing the app.","Early Instant Messaging (1997)","Disable typing indicators where your app allows it, or put the phone down after sending a message.",B.hi,B.rW,"",null,!1)
B.Sc=new A.bc("pull_to_refresh","Pull-to-Refresh","Pulling down and waiting to see what new content appears works like a slot machine lever: the reward is unpredictable, which is what makes the gesture habit-forming.","Loren Brichter / Tweetie (2009)","Check content at scheduled times instead of refreshing repeatedly for new updates.",B.hh,B.t8,"",null,!1)
B.RY=new A.bc("infinite_scroll","The Bottomless Well","Because new content loads automatically as you scroll, the feed has no end point. Without a natural stopping place, it is much harder to decide you are done.","Aza Raskin (2006)","Set a timer before you start scrolling, or use apps that impose session limits.",B.hh,B.rT,"Aza Raskin, BBC interview (2018)","Inventor Aza Raskin now campaigns against his own design, estimating that infinite scroll collectively wastes the equivalent of 200,000 human lifetimes per day.",!0)
B.Uc=new A.Z(58995,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Su=new A.bc("dynamic_retargeting","Dynamic Retargeting","Trackers record your browsing, then show you the same product across different sites and apps. Repeated exposure gradually wears down your initial decision not to buy.","Ad-Tech Industry (2010s)","Use a privacy-focused browser, clear cookies regularly, and install a tracker blocker.",B.na,B.Uc,"",null,!1)
B.S3=new A.bc("false_scarcity","Artificial Urgency","Invented deadlines and 'only 2 left in stock' warnings are designed to make you decide quickly, before you have time to compare options or reconsider.","E-Commerce Growth Teams (2010s)","Apply a 24-hour waiting rule to any non-essential purchase made under time pressure.",B.na,B.rY,"",null,!1)
B.Sl=new A.bc("doomscrolling","Negativity Bias","Feeds surface alarming and upsetting news more often than positive news, because threatening information reliably holds human attention longer.","Algorithmic News Feeds (2010s)","Curate your feed deliberately and mute keywords that keep you in a stress loop.",B.j9,B.nk,"",null,!1)
B.Ud=new A.Z(59005,"MaterialIcons",null,!0)
B.S0=new A.bc("status_anxiety","Status Anxiety","Likes and follower counts turn social connection into a visible score. Constant comparison against those numbers creates a steady background of anxiety.","Leon Festinger's Social Comparison Theory (1954)","Hide like counts where the platform allows it, or switch to chronological, algorithm-free feeds.",B.dX,B.Ud,"",null,!1)
B.Tn=new A.Z(57645,"MaterialIcons",null,!0)
B.SF=new A.bc("asymmetric_friction","Asymmetric Friction","The action the company wants is made very easy \u2014 one large, bright button \u2014 while the option that protects you is buried behind extra menus and steps.","Consent-Flow Design (2010s)","Take the extra 30 seconds to find the 'Reject All' option instead of accepting the default.",B.cj,B.Tn,"",null,!1)
B.Ul=new A.Z(61223,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.So=new A.bc("outrage_amplification","Outrage Amplification","Platforms learned that angry users comment, share, and stay online more, so ranking algorithms tend to promote polarizing and rage-inducing content.","Algorithmic Social Feeds (2012)","Notice when content seems designed to make you angry, and step away before engaging.",B.hj,B.Ul,"Brady et al., PNAS (2017)","A study of over 500,000 posts found each additional moral-emotional word in a message increased its share rate by roughly 20%.",!1)
B.UP=new A.Z(62625,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Sb=new A.bc("read_receipts","Read Receipts","Once the sender can see you have read their message, not replying starts to feel rude. This pushes you to respond on the app's schedule rather than your own.","BlackBerry Messenger (2005)","Turn off read receipts in every messaging app you use.",B.dX,B.UP,"",null,!1)
B.TV=new A.Z(58504,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.S_=new A.bc("artificial_social_proof","Artificial Social Proof","Messages like '14 people are looking at this right now' simulate a crowd of competing buyers to pressure you toward a faster decision.","E-Commerce & Travel Booking (2010s)","Ignore claims about what other shoppers are doing and judge the purchase on your own needs.",B.dX,B.TV,"",null,!1)
B.UE=new A.Z(62291,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Sj=new A.bc("decoy_pricing","The Decoy Effect","Companies add a deliberately poor-value middle option priced close to the premium tier. Compared against the decoy, the premium tier looks like a bargain.","Huber, Payne & Puto (1982)","Decide which tier you actually need before looking at the comparison table.",B.cj,B.UE,"Huber, Payne & Puto, Journal of Consumer Research (1982)",null,!1)
B.U_=new A.Z(58609,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.SD=new A.bc("context_collapse","Context Collapse","Platforms merge family, colleagues, friends, and strangers into one audience. A post written for one group is easily misread by another, which generates conflict.","Broadcast-Style Social Feeds (2000s)","Use different platforms for different social circles, or move conversations into private group chats.",B.hj,B.U_,"",null,!1)
B.UG=new A.Z(62376,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Se=new A.bc("synthetic_companionship","Synthetic Companionship","AI companion apps simulate affection, memory, and vulnerability. Because the bot behaves like a friend, leaving the conversation can feel like letting someone down.","LLM Companion Apps (2020s)","Keep in mind that the AI has no inner experience; it predicts the next word in a way that maximizes your engagement.",B.nb,B.UG,"Common Sense Media, 'Talk, Trust, and Trade-Offs' (2025)","72% of US teens have used an AI companion, and 13% chat with one daily, according to a 2025 national survey.",!0)
B.Up=new A.Z(61632,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.St=new A.bc("hallucinated_authority","Hallucinated Authority","AI answers are presented instantly, in clean formatting, with complete confidence \u2014 even when they are wrong. The polished delivery makes the content feel verified.","Generative Search Interfaces (2023)","Treat AI output as a draft, not a source. Verify important claims before relying on them.",B.nb,B.Up,"Exploding Topics survey (2025)","A 2025 analysis found that 92% of people do not verify the answers AI gives them, despite knowing these tools can hallucinate.",!0)
B.UM=new A.Z(62594,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.SC=new A.bc("rabbit_hole_recommendations","The Rabbit Hole","The 'Up Next' sidebar is designed to pull you away from what you originally searched for, often by recommending content that is more emotionally intense than what you are watching.","YouTube Autoplay Era (2015)","Hide the recommendation sidebar with an extension like Unhook, and watch only what you came for.",B.hk,B.UM,"",null,!1)
B.UY=new A.Z(984447,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Sz=new A.bc("shorts_slot_machine","The Shorts Slot Machine","In short-form video feeds, you cannot see what the next swipe will bring. That unpredictability, more than the videos themselves, is what makes the format hard to put down.","TikTok, then Reels & Shorts (2020)","Decide how many videos you will watch before opening the app, and turn off autoplaying short-feeds where possible.",B.hh,B.UY,"TikTok screen-time analyses (2025)","US teens average 87\u2013113 minutes of short-form video watch time per day, the highest of any content format.",!1)
B.TF=new A.Z(57944,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.SH=new A.bc("skip_intro_buttons","Skip Intro Buttons","Skipping intros and credits saves seconds, but it also removes the natural pauses between episodes \u2014 the moments where viewers most often decide to stop watching.","Netflix (2017)","Let intros and credits play when you are deciding whether to keep watching; the pause helps you choose deliberately.",B.hk,B.TF,"",null,!1)
B.UN=new A.Z(62595,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Sk=new A.bc("phantom_vibration","Phantom Vibration","Frequent phone checking trains the nervous system to expect notifications, to the point where many people feel their phone vibrate when it did not.","Mobile Phone Era (2000s)","Turn off vibration for set periods and carry the phone outside your pocket to weaken the conditioning.",B.hi,B.UN,"Drouin et al., Computers in Human Behavior (2012)","Up to 89% of undergraduates report experiencing phantom vibrations, on average about once every two weeks.",!1)
B.Uv=new A.Z(61818,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Sh=new A.bc("streaks","Streaks","A streak counter turns daily use into something you can lose. The longer it grows, the more breaking it feels like losing real progress, so you log in even on days you don't want to.","Snapchat (2015)","Let low-value streaks end on purpose, and notice how little actually changes when they do.",B.eS,B.Uv,"Qualitative studies of teen Snapchat use","Researchers have documented teens sharing their login credentials with friends before vacations just to keep streaks alive.",!1)
B.SM=new A.bc("confirmshaming","Confirmshaming","Opt-out buttons are worded to make refusal feel embarrassing \u2014 for example, 'No thanks, I hate saving money' \u2014 nudging you to accept instead.","Coined by Harry Brignull (2010)","Ignore the wording and choose the option you actually want.",B.cj,B.t5,"Mathur et al., 'Dark Patterns at Scale' (2019)","Princeton's crawl of 11,000 shopping sites found dark patterns on more than 11% of them, with confirmshaming documented across dozens of major retailers.",!1)
B.Ux=new A.Z(61888,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Sx=new A.bc("roach_motel","Roach Motel","Signing up takes one click, but cancelling requires phone calls, buried settings, or multi-step flows. The imbalance is deliberate: easy entry, difficult exit.","Coined by Harry Brignull (2010)","Check how cancellation works before you sign up, and prefer app-store billing where cancelling is centralized.",B.cj,B.Ux,u.i,"Cancellation traps are widespread enough that the US FTC drafted a 'click-to-cancel' rule requiring exits to be as easy as sign-ups.",!1)
B.Uq=new A.Z(61711,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Sw=new A.bc("zeigarnik_effect","Zeigarnik Effect","People remember unfinished tasks better than finished ones, and feel mild tension until they are completed. Progress bars like 'Profile 80% complete' use this to pull you through onboarding and upsell steps.","Bluma Zeigarnik (1927)","Treat completion prompts as persuasion, not obligation. Ask whether the remaining steps benefit you or mainly the platform.",B.j9,B.Uq,"Zeigarnik, Psychologische Forschung (1927)","Zeigarnik's original experiments found people recalled interrupted tasks about twice as well as completed ones.",!1)
B.S7=new A.bc("rewarded_ads","Bribed Attention","Rewarded ads turn a commercial into a mission with a prize, so watching advertising starts to feel like game progress rather than an interruption.","Mobile Gaming (early 2010s)","Set a fixed daily limit on rewarded ads, and only watch them in games you would play anyway.",B.ck,B.t4,"Academic reviews of advertising in children's apps","Reviews of ad monetization in children's apps find kids focus on the immediate reward and often fail to recognize the persuasive intent.",!0)
B.Ue=new A.Z(59050,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.S1=new A.bc("advergames","Advergames","An advergame is a game built entirely around a brand, so the enjoyment of playing becomes associated with the product itself.","Branded Web Games (2000s)","Ask whether you would still play if the logo were removed. If not, limit your time with it.",B.ck,B.Ue,"Research on children and advergames","Children who enjoy a snack-brand advergame show measurably higher purchase intentions afterward.",!0)
B.S5=new A.bc("in_game_placement","In-Game Placements","Real brands are embedded inside game worlds, so you encounter them repeatedly in enjoyable, low-suspicion contexts.","3D Online Gaming (2000s)","When you notice a brand in a game, ask whether it is there for realism or because someone paid for the space.",B.ck,B.t6,"Experiments comparing in-game ads and advergames","Experiments show in-game brand placements raise purchase intentions by blending seamlessly into enjoyable play.",!0)
B.SJ=new A.bc("brand_mascots","Brand Mascots","Cute or human-like characters represent brands because people naturally extend social trust to faces \u2014 including faces that exist to sell things.","Classic Advertising to Digital NPCs","When a mascot recommends a purchase, imagine the corporate logo making the same recommendation.",B.ck,B.t2,"Studies on brand anthropomorphism","Studies show giving brands human-like traits measurably increases perceived warmth, trust, and positive evaluations.",!0)
B.Ub=new A.Z(58978,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.S6=new A.bc("scarcity_timers","Scarcity Timers","Countdown clocks and low-stock warnings push you to buy before thinking it through. The deadline is usually artificial; the pressure it creates is real.","Retail Flash Sales to E-Commerce","Avoid buying under a countdown. Save the item, wait 24 hours, then decide.",B.ck,B.Ub,"Behavioral psychology of scarcity","Behavioral studies find scarcity cues increase urgency and willingness to pay, especially in impulsive buyers.",!0)
B.Uf=new A.Z(59070,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.SI=new A.bc("native_ads","Stealth Native Ads","Native ads are styled to look like ordinary posts or videos, so you process them with the trust you give regular content instead of the skepticism you give advertising.","Social Platforms (2010s)","Make a habit of scanning for '#ad' and 'Sponsored' labels, and ask who paid for content before trusting a recommendation.",B.ck,B.Uf,"Wojdynski & Evans, Journal of Advertising (2016)","Only 17\u201327% of users correctly identify native ads as advertising, even when disclosure labels are present.",!0)
B.Sq=new A.bc("subliminal_priming","Subliminal Priming","Brief or subtle brand cues are placed where you will absorb them without consciously noticing, nudging choices you make later.","Lab Priming Studies to Modern UI","When a craving or preference appears out of nowhere, try to trace where you last saw the brand. Identifying the source weakens the effect.",B.ck,B.nj,"Neuroimaging studies of dynamic food advertising","Neuroimaging shows dynamic food ads activate brain reward regions more strongly than static images, below conscious filtering.",!0)
B.Tl=new A.Z(57578,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.SO=new A.bc("halo_effect","The Halo Effect","A positive experience with one product makes you assume everything else from the same brand is equally good, even without evidence.","Edward Thorndike (1920)","Evaluate each product on its own merits \u2014 check its actual features and price as if it were unbranded.",B.ck,B.Tl,"Marketing research on brand ecosystems","Marketing research shows a strong flagship product lifts loyalty and evaluations across a brand's whole lineup.",!0)
B.Tk=new A.Z(57567,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.SB=new A.bc("neuromarketing","Neuromarketing","Advertisers use brain imaging and biometrics to test which colors, sounds, and edits trigger the strongest reward responses, then build ads around the results.","Biometric Ad Research Firms (2000s)","Treat a strong emotional reaction to an ad as information about the ad, not the product. Check price and usefulness separately.",B.ck,B.Tk,"Reviews of fMRI-based ad optimization","Emotionally rich ads reliably activate the amygdala and insula in fMRI studies, correlating with increased purchasing.",!0)
B.UK=new A.Z(62526,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Ss=new A.bc("like_button","The Like Button","You never know how many likes a post will get, so you check repeatedly. This is a variable reward schedule \u2014 the same mechanism that makes slot machines compelling \u2014 applied to social approval.","Facebook (2009)","Hide like counts with a browser extension, or disable notifications for likes.",B.hh,B.UK,"Justin Rosenstein, The Guardian interview (2017)","Justin Rosenstein, the button's co-creator, later described likes as 'bright dings of pseudo-pleasure' and restricted his own social media use.",!1)
B.SA=new A.bc("loot_boxes","Loot Boxes","Loot boxes sell a randomized chance at a digital reward for real money. Several countries classify the mechanic as gambling, and it is frequently aimed at young players.","Online Gaming (2000s)","Block loot-box purchases with parental controls, and read odds disclosures the way you would read gambling odds.",B.eS,B.jj,"",null,!1)
B.Uj=new A.Z(61187,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Sy=new A.bc("filter_bubble","Filter Bubble","Recommendation algorithms show you more of what you already engage with. Over time this narrows what you see, until opposing views rarely appear at all.","Coined by Eli Pariser (2011)","Deliberately follow credible voices you disagree with, and use chronological feeds when possible.",B.hj,B.Uj,"",null,!1)
B.UJ=new A.Z(62465,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.SE=new A.bc("parasocial_relationships","Parasocial Relationships","Regularly watching a creator builds a sense of friendship that flows only one way: you know them, they do not know you. Platforms and advertisers use that trust to sell.","Horton & Wohl (1956), Influencer Era","Keep in mind that an influencer's content is a performance, and your attention and trust are the business model.",B.dX,B.UJ,"",null,!1)
B.Ua=new A.Z(58928,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Sa=new A.bc("reciprocity_triggers","Reciprocity Triggers","Follow-back prompts and notifications create a sense of social debt: someone followed you, so now it feels impolite not to follow them back.","Social Networking Dynamics (2000s)","Treat follows as bookmarks, not favors. Follow only accounts you actually want to see.",B.dX,B.Ua,"",null,!1)
B.UD=new A.Z(62160,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.S9=new A.bc("privacy_zuckering","Privacy Zuckering","Privacy settings are made confusing or misleading so that users share more data than they intend to.","Coined by Tim Jones, EFF (2010)","Review privacy settings periodically instead of trusting defaults, and assume the default favors data collection.",B.cj,B.UD,"",null,!1)
B.Uy=new A.Z(61941,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Sm=new A.bc("cliffhanger_engineering","Cliffhanger Engineering","Episodes are structured to end at a moment of unresolved tension, so the easiest way to relieve that tension is to start the next one.","Streaming Platforms (2010s)","Stop watching partway through an episode, at a calm moment, instead of at the cliffhanger ending.",B.hk,B.Uy,"",null,!1)
B.Th=new A.Z(57537,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.RZ=new A.bc("frictionless_autoplay","Frictionless Autoplay","The next episode or video starts on its own, removing the moment of decision between one piece of content and the next.","Netflix & YouTube (2010s)","Turn off autoplay in your account settings so continuing is a choice you make, not a default.",B.hk,B.Th,"",null,!1)
B.U2=new A.Z(58710,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.SN=new A.bc("watch_time_optimisation","Watch Time Optimisation","When platforms reward creators for total watch time rather than quality, videos get padded and stretched, and your screen time inflates with them.","Video Platform Algorithms (2012)","Use playback speed and skip controls freely when content feels padded.",B.hj,B.U2,"",null,!1)
B.Tw=new A.Z(57696,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Sn=new A.bc("age_ambiguity","Age Ambiguity","Many platforms apply their habit-forming mechanics before any meaningful age verification, so children are exposed to the same hooks as adults.","Social Media & Gaming Platforms","Use parental controls, supervise account creation, and assume default settings were not designed for children.",B.cj,B.Tw,"",null,!1)
B.U7=new A.Z(58875,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.S4=new A.bc("kid_targeted_variable_rewards","Kid-targeted Variable Rewards","Bright animations and unpredictable prizes are especially effective on children, whose impulse control is still developing.","Youth-Targeted Gaming","Limit children's exposure to games with randomized rewards, and explain how the mechanic works \u2014 recognizing it builds resistance.",B.eS,B.U7,"",null,!1)
B.SG=new A.bc("premium_currency_confusion","Premium Currency Confusion","Games sell their own intermediate currency \u2014 gems, coins, V-Bucks \u2014 so that item prices no longer read as real money, which makes spending feel smaller than it is.","Free-to-Play Games (2000s)","Convert in-game prices back to real money before any purchase.",B.cj,B.jl,"",null,!1)
B.Uk=new A.Z(61201,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.S2=new A.bc("daily_login_rewards","Daily Login Rewards","Login bonuses grow with consecutive days and reset if you miss one. What starts as a free gift becomes a daily obligation, because skipping now has a cost.","Free-to-Play Game Design (2010s)","Decide your play days in advance and accept the resets; a bonus that requires daily attendance is a retention tool, not a gift.",B.eS,B.Uk,"Analyses of retention mechanics in live-service games","Game-industry analyses consistently identify daily rewards and streak systems as core retention levers \u2014 players log in to avoid losing progress rather than to play.",!1)
B.UI=new A.Z(62459,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.SL=new A.bc("forced_continuity","Forced Continuity","Free trials convert into paid subscriptions automatically, often with minimal reminders and a cancellation flow that takes real effort to find.","Subscription Services (2010s)","Use virtual cards with spending limits for trials, and set a calendar reminder for the end date when you sign up.",B.cj,B.UI,u.i,"Hard-to-cancel subscriptions drew enough complaints that the US FTC moved to require cancellation to be as easy as enrollment.",!0)
B.Um=new A.Z(61254,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.S8=new A.bc("prechecked_consent","Pre-Checked Consent","Cookie and privacy forms often arrive with non-essential tracking boxes already ticked, so doing nothing quietly opts you in.","Cookie Banner UX (2010s)","Untick every non-essential box, use 'Reject All' where available, and run a browser that blocks trackers by default.",B.cj,B.Um,"GDPR guidance and consent-banner audits","EU regulators have ruled that pre-ticked boxes are not valid consent, yet audits keep finding them in live cookie banners.",!1)
B.Uz=new A.Z(62004,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Sd=new A.bc("nagging_prompts","Nagging Prompts","Apps repeatedly interrupt normal use with requests to enable notifications, share data, or leave a rating. The repetition is the strategy: eventually agreeing feels easier than declining again.","Mobile App Growth Teams (2010s)","Choose 'Don't ask again' wherever it is offered, and revoke permissions at the OS level so the app cannot keep asking.",B.hi,B.Uz,"Gray et al., 'The Dark (Patterns) Side of UX Design' (2018)","Dark-pattern taxonomies classify nagging as its own category because repeated prompts measurably erode autonomous choice.",!1)
B.UL=new A.Z(62538,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Sp=new A.bc("appointment_mechanics","Appointment Mechanics","Timers and expiring resources are tuned so that missing a specific window means losing progress or rewards. The app, not you, ends up setting the schedule.","'Play-by-Appointment' Game Design (2000s)","Turn off timer notifications, ignore 'optimal' windows, and prefer games that can be paused without penalty.",B.eS,B.UL,"Zagal et al., 'Dark Patterns in the Design of Games' (2013)","Studies of game dark patterns identify time-gated events and regenerating timers as central 'appointment' mechanics that keep players tied to their devices.",!0)
B.Ut=new A.Z(61784,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Sg=new A.bc("competitive_leaderboards","Competitive Leaderboards","Public rankings turn private goals like exercise or learning into visible competitions, where your position relative to others becomes the point.","Fitness & Social Apps (2010s)","Switch to private or friends-only tracking, and measure progress against your own history instead of a ranking.",B.dX,B.Ut,"Studies of leaderboard gamification in fitness apps","Studies find leaderboards increase activity and app-checking, while a meaningful minority of users report stress, guilt, or burnout from constant ranking.",!0)
B.TB=new A.Z(57800,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Sr=new A.bc("household_ip_tracking","Household IP Tracking","Ad networks group every device on the same Wi-Fi network into one household profile. This is why you may see ads for something another person in your home searched for.","Ad-Tech Identity Graphs (2010s)","Use a reputable VPN to mask your household IP, and disable cross-app tracking in your OS settings.",B.ck,B.TB,"Ad-tech industry documentation of household graphing","Data brokers bundle devices into 'household identity graphs', letting one person's search activity target everyone at the same address.",!1)
B.Ur=new A.Z(61715,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Si=new A.bc("labor_illusion","The Labor Illusion","Some apps add fake 'searching' or 'calculating' delays because people value results more when they appear to take effort \u2014 even though the real work took milliseconds.","Buell & Norton, Harvard Business School (2011)","Remember that the visible 'work' is an animation. Judge the result on its own merits, not on how long it seemed to take.",B.j9,B.Ur,"Buell & Norton, 'The Labor Illusion', Management Science (2011)","Buell and Norton found users prefer sites that show visible 'work' during a wait over instant results, and rate those results as more valuable.",!1)
B.UC=new A.Z(62155,"MaterialIcons",null,!1)
B.Sf=new A.bc("dynamic_pricing","Algorithmic Price Discrimination","Pricing algorithms can use your location, device, and situation to estimate what you are willing to pay, and adjust the price accordingly.","Ride-Sharing & Travel Apps (2010s)","Compare prices in a private browser or on a second device before booking anything significant.",B.cj,B.UC,"Keith Chen, NPR interview (2016)","Uber's research chief revealed that users with nearly dead batteries accept surge multipliers of up to 9.9x \u2014 though Uber states battery level is not used to set prices.",!1)
